,title,link,text,language,family
0,Malayic languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malayic_languages,"The Malayic languages are a branch of the Malayo-Polynesian subgroup of the Austronesian language family. [1] The most prominent member is Malay, a pluricentric language given national status in Brunei and Singapore while also providing the basis for national standards Malaysian in Malaysia and Indonesian in Indonesia. [2][3] The Malayic branch also includes local languages spoken by ethnic ...",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
1,Malayic Dayak languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malayic_Dayak_languages,"Malayic Dayak is a dialect chain of Malayic [2] spoken in West Kalimantan (North Kayong, Ketapang, Kapuas Hulu, Melawi, and Sintang) and the western part of Central Kalimantan (Lamandau, Sukamara, West Kotawaringin, Seruyan, and East Kotawaringin).",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
2,Dayak Music of Kalimantan - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLnOSH5j1sQh-UMDC5dfqd--ERg_4kRQ09,Major groups of the Dayak include the Kenyah and the Iban. Read Wikipedia's article on the Dayaks for information about the interesting religion of these people.,Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
3,Music Etnic Dayak - YouTube,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0z40mqDcMr0,"Irama ini bukan sekadar hiburan, tetapi juga ungkapan jiwa, doa, dan hubungan dengan alam serta leluhur/ Dayak instrumental music brings the unique harmony of Borneo through the strings of the ...",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
4,"Music of Indonesia, Vol. 17: Kalimantan: Dayak Ritual and Festival ...",https://folkways.si.edu/music-of-indonesia-vol-17-kalimantan-dayak-ritual-and-festival-music/islamica-world/album/smithsonian,"Our second album on Kalimantan (Indonesian Borneo) presents music from seven Dayak groups, three (out of four) provinces, and a variety of genres and ensembles. Many selections are devoted to the gong ensembles of Kalimantan, which are unlike either the large gamelan of Java and Bali or the …",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
5,Malayic Dayak languages — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/malayic_dayak_languages,"The Malayic Dayak languages constitute a subgroup of the Malayic branch within the Austronesian language family, spoken primarily by non-Muslim Dayak communities in the interior of Borneo. These languages, which include prominent members such as Iban and Kendayan (also known as Salako), exhibit close lexical and structural affinities to standard Malay while displaying distinct phonological ...",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
6,Traditional Dayak Music & Instruments Of The Indigenous Tribes Of ...,https://wild-eden.bandcamp.com/album/traditional-dayak-music-instruments-of-the-indigenous-tribes-of-borneo-island,"Traditional Dayak Music & Instruments Of The Indigenous Tribes Of Borneo Island by Hatangku Hakangkalu, Dede Purna Budaya, Syaer Sua, released 27 May 2019 1. 01 Warrior Dance Music 2. 02 Giring-Giring (Happiness Dance, Welcoming People to Borneo Island) 3.",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
7,Traditional music of the Dayak Kanayatn: shaping cultural identity amid ...,https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/13504630.2026.2664576,ABSTRACT This study provides an in-depth examination of the cultural identity challenges faced by the Dayak Kanayatn community as the existence of their traditional music declines under the pressures of globalization and cultural modernization.,Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
8,Kenyah songs research by Pek Lin Chong,https://peklinkenyah.com/,"Describes 20 years of research in the folk songs of Borneo by Malaysian ethnomusicologist Chong Pek Lin, focusing on the musical talents of the Kenyah of Sarawak. The Kenyah, an indigenous Dayak group are celebrated for their multipart choral singing, instruments such as the sape and graceful dances such as kanjet laki.",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
9,On the syntax of West Kalimantan: Asymmetries and A'-movement in ...,https://mavmatrix.uta.edu/linguistics_tesol_dissertations/113/,"This dissertation focuses on five Malayic and five Land Dayak languages of West Kalimantan, Indonesia, exploring voice, A'-movement, and extraction asymmetries through a Minimalist framework. The main goal of this dissertation is to bridge the gap between documentation and syntactic analysis in a few ways: a) by using data from all underdocumented, and several undocumented languages; b) by ...",Malayic Dayak,Austronesian
